UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 3, Lesson 3: Verb tense and adverbs', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Use past and present tense consistently and choose adverbs that clarify actions.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Keep the independent response and two written check responses; record oral retelling or performance where required by the task.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Create a new example using a completely different character or setting, show the same skill, then explain to another person how you checked its accuracy.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"WE DO — attempt each prompt before comparing to the feedback.\n1. Change \"The girl jumps over the puddle\" to past tense.\nCOMPARE: \"The girl jumped over the puddle\" moves the action to the past.\n2. Repair \"Yesterday we walk to the creek and found smooth stones\".\nCOMPARE: \"Yesterday we walked to the creek and found smooth stones.\" The two main verbs are past tense.\n3. What is the adverb in \"The children waited patiently\"? What does it explain?\nCOMPARE: Patiently tells how the children waited; it adds to the meaning of the verb waited.\n4. Improve \"The driver stopped\" to show that stopping was cautious and happened yesterday.\nCOMPARE: \"Yesterday the driver stopped carefully\" is clear; slowly or safely could fit another context.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"YOU DO — complete the following substantial original task, not a copied model.\nCorrect the tense in \"Yesterday Ben runs to the jetty and finds a lost glove.\" Then write four connected past-tense sentences about helping at a garden. Include two useful adverbs, underline every main verb and explain one adverb choice. Revise any accidental switches to present tense.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"English","learning_intention":"Use past and present tense consistently and choose adverbs that clarify actions.","lesson_number":3,"lesson_role":"Language and spelling","materials":["Paper or typing area","Pencil for optional evidence"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper to copy only the original independent prompt; have the child respond in writing or orally as required. Save a photograph or brief parent observation for the learning record.","outcome_codes":["EN2-CWT-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Correct the tense in \"Yesterday Ben runs to the jetty and finds a lost glove.\" Then write four connected past-tense sentences about helping at a garden. Include two useful adverbs, underline every main verb and explain o Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: \"The girl jumped over the puddle\" moves the action to the past. Reasoning: I used tense by attending to past and point to the words or events th rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Not every past-tense verb ends in ed; write went, not goed. Correction: \"The girl jumped over the puddle\" moves the action to the past. Why: the correction restores accurate use of tense.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Not every past-tense verb ends in ed; write went, not goed. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating tense on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with tense, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"\"The girl jumped over the puddle\" moves the action to the past. This matches the lesson skill for tense.","options":["Copy the worked example and call it independent work.","\"The girl jumped over the puddle\" moves the action to the past.","Not every past-tense verb ends in ed; write went, not goed.","Ignore the task and invent an unrelated answer."],"question":"Repair \"Yesterday we walk to the creek and found smooth stones\". Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Not every past-tense verb ends in ed; write went, not goed. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Not every past-tense verb ends in ed; write went, not goed.","Use past and present tense consistently and choose adverbs that clarify actions.","\"The girl jumped over the puddle\" moves the action to the past.","Careful use of past and point to the words or events th with clear evidence."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising tense?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named past and point to the words or events th, not by copying the model.","options":["Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Treat a missing detail as a proven fact.","Apply the idea with new evidence and name the past and point to the words or events th used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence."],"question":"A learner is practising tense. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The skill is shown by quoting or pointing to wording that carries tense.","options":["Point to exact words in the sentence that show tense and say what they do.","Ignore the sentence and invent a more exciting event.","Say the writing is good without naming any words.","Not every past-tense verb ends in ed; write went, not goed."],"question":"Read: \"Under the pier, Sam found a locked tin and a dripping map.\" Which choice best uses tense from this lesson?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The correct statement applies the language skill; the others miss the evidence.","options":["Not every past-tense verb ends in ed; write went, not goed.","Ignore the task and invent an unrelated answer.","Copy the worked example and call it independent work.","\"The girl jumped over the puddle\" moves the action to the past."],"question":"Which statement correctly applies the lesson idea about tense?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Start from a specific textual clue, then explain its effect.","options":["Guess from the title alone with no textual clue.","Copy a model sentence from another story unchanged.","Treat a missing detail as a proven fact.","Locate a specific word, phrase or structural clue before deciding."],"question":"In a new text, what should you do first to practise past and point to the words or events th?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Repair means fixing meaning or evidence, not padding.","options":["Add adjectives without fixing the wrong idea.","Make the answer longer while keeping the same mistake.","Delete the evidence that disagreed with your first guess.","Change the inaccurate idea or missing evidence, not only the length."],"question":"Which revision actually repairs meaning?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Transfer needs fresh material with an explained language choice.","options":["Use a fresh sentence or text and explain one precise choice about tense.","Retype the worked model as the final answer.","Change fonts and claim the work is new.","Not every past-tense verb ends in ed; write went, not goed."],"question":"Which response shows transfer of tense to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating tense on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: \"The girl jumped over the puddle\" moves the action to the past. Reasoning: I used tense by attending to past and point to the words or events th rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with tense, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Not every past-tense verb ends in ed; write went, not goed. Correction: \"The girl jumped over the puddle\" moves the action to the past. Why: the correction restores accurate use of tense.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Submit your original independent work and your brief explanation of the key choices you made.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-eng-y3-w03-l3","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA alignment; compare the existing Coming Soon card and official syllabus content points before marking verified.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise tense.","Read the mission before you start writing or speaking.","Your goal is: Use past and present tense consistently and choose adverbs that clarify actions."],"guided":["Attempt each guided prompt without looking ahead.","Compare your attempt with the supplied specific feedback.","Guided comparison: \"Yesterday we walked to the creek and found smooth stones.\" The two main verbs are past tense."],"learn":["Study the first worked demonstration carefully.","Name the exact words or events that make it work.","Demonstration: \"The possum climbs\" describes what it does now or regularly. \"The possum climbed\" tells a past event. \"The possum went\" is past although it "],"quiz":["Read the whole question and eliminate misconceptions.","Prefer the option that quotes or uses actual evidence.","Likely trap: Not every word ending ly is an adverb, and not every adverb ends in ly."],"submit":["Include your own response and a short reason.","Proofread names, sequence and punctuation before sending.","Success looks like: Use past and present tense consistently and choose adverbs that clarify actions."],"teach0":["Focus on locate the time of the action.","Hold this teaching point: Verb tense helps a reader know whether something happens now or happened earlier. Regular past verbs often end in -ed, b","Worked comparison: \"The girl jumped over the puddle\" moves the action to the past."],"teach1":["Focus on keep the time consistent across a paragraph.","Hold this teaching point: When telling past events, choose past tense for the main actions unless there is a clear reason for a time shift.","Worked comparison: \"Yesterday we walked to the creek and found smooth stones.\" The two main verbs are past tense."],"teach2":["Focus on adverbs can explain how, when or where.","Hold this teaching point: An adverb often gives more information about a verb. Many end in -ly, but not all do; some words work differently in dif","Worked comparison: Patiently tells how the children waited; it adds to the meaning of the verb waited."],"teach3":["Focus on revise for accurate meaning, not adjective clutter.","Hold this teaching point: Choose one useful adverb when it adds information. Check tense before polishing description.","Worked comparison: \"Yesterday the driver stopped carefully\" is clear; slowly or safely could fit another context."],"write":["Use the independent instructions, not the model words.","Give an original response and the reason behind it.","Independent reminder: Correct the tense in \"Yesterday Ben runs to the jetty and finds a lost glove.\" Then write four connected past-tense sentences about helping at a garden. Include"]},"subject":"English","success_criteria":["I can use tense with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify past and point to the words or events that support it.","I can practise present independently on a new prompt.","I can notice adverb needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The best approach uses the specific concept, evidence and reasoning before practising independently; copying or inventing unsupported facts does not show understanding. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["Add a made-up event and describe it as a fact.","Ignore the task and choose an unrelated detail.","Use the example and explain the clue or language choice before trying tense independently.","Copy every sentence exactly without explaining it."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about tense?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WATCH MY THINKING\n\"The possum climbs\" describes what it does now or regularly. \"The possum climbed\" tells a past event. \"The possum went\" is past although it does not end in ed.\n\nYOUR SMALL TRY\nChange \"The girl jumps over the puddle\" to past tense.\n\nCOMPARE AND CORRECT\n\"The girl jumped over the puddle\" moves the action to the past.","explain":"Verb tense helps a reader know whether something happens now or happened earlier. Regular past verbs often end in -ed, but many are irregular.\n\nVerb tense helps a reader know whether something happens now or happened earlier. Regular past verbs often end in -ed, but many are irregular. This matters because a reader or listener can only understand your ideas when you notice how the language works, test your understanding on specific examples and explain the evidence for your choice. Keep the meaning of the original text rather than changing facts to make an answer sound impressive. Read the model closely: which particular word, event or detail changes the meaning? Try explaining the same decision to someone else without repeating the model sentence. Then compare your new attempt with the feedback and revise anything that does not fit the actual evidence.","icon":"📖","notice":"WATCH OUT\nNot every past-tense verb ends in ed; write went, not goed. Re-read the prompt and correct the misunderstanding before moving on.","title":"Locate the time of the action","visual":[{"alt":"Present tense climbs beside past tense climbed for the same possum action.","caption":"Tense tells whether the action happens now or already happened.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Present</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">The possum climbs</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Past</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">The possum climbed</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I read the first example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the exact words or events that carry the meaning before you copy a conclusion.","who":"juno"},{"text":"So I should try the small prompt myself, then compare it with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: \"The possum climbs\" describes what it does now or regularly. \"The possum climbed\" tells a past event. \"The possum went\"  Then write your own attempt.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I read the first example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.\nJuno: Name the exact words or events that carry the meaning before you copy a conclusion.\nPip: So I should try the small prompt myself, then compare it with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: \"The possum climbs\" describes what it does now or regularly. \"The possum climbed\" tells a past event. \"The possum went\"  Then write your own attempt."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The best approach uses the specific concept, evidence and reasoning before practising independently; copying or inventing unsupported facts does not show understanding. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["Copy every sentence exactly without explaining it.","Add a made-up event and describe it as a fact.","Ignore the task and choose an unrelated detail.","Use the example and explain the clue or language choice before trying past independently."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about tense?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WATCH MY THINKING\nDraft: \"Mira opened the gate and sees a fox.\" Both actions happened in the story''s past, so I revise: \"Mira opened the gate and saw a fox.\"\n\nYOUR SMALL TRY\nRepair \"Yesterday we walk to the creek and found smooth stones\".\n\nCOMPARE AND CORRECT\n\"Yesterday we walked to the creek and found smooth stones.\" The two main verbs are past tense.","explain":"When telling past events, choose past tense for the main actions unless there is a clear reason for a time shift.\n\nWhen telling past events, choose past tense for the main actions unless there is a clear reason for a time shift. This matters because a reader or listener can only understand your ideas when you notice how the language works, test your understanding on specific examples and explain the evidence for your choice. Keep the meaning of the original text rather than changing facts to make an answer sound impressive. Read the model closely: which particular word, event or detail changes the meaning? Try explaining the same decision to someone else without repeating the model sentence. Then compare your new attempt with the feedback and revise anything that does not fit the actual evidence.","icon":"📖","notice":"WATCH OUT\nDo not change only one verb and leave others in the wrong time. Re-read the prompt and correct the misunderstanding before moving on.","title":"Keep the time consistent across a paragraph","visual":[{"alt":"Inconsistent sees corrected to saw so both actions stay in the past.","caption":"Keep the main verbs in the same time unless there is a clear reason to shift.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Mixed draft</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">opened the gate and sees a fox</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Consistent past</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">opened the gate and saw a fox</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The best approach uses the specific concept, evidence and reasoning before practising independently; copying or inventing unsupported facts does not show understanding. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["Add a made-up event and describe it as a fact.","Ignore the task and choose an unrelated detail.","Use the example and explain the clue or language choice before trying present independently.","Copy every sentence exactly without explaining it."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about tense?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WATCH MY THINKING\n\"The cyclist pedalled carefully\" explains how. \"Soon the cyclist rested\" explains when. \"The cyclist rode outside\" tells where. An adverb need not end in ly.\n\nYOUR SMALL TRY\nWhat is the adverb in \"The children waited patiently\"? What does it explain?\n\nCOMPARE AND CORRECT\nPatiently tells how the children waited; it adds to the meaning of the verb waited.","explain":"An adverb often gives more information about a verb. Many end in -ly, but not all do; some words work differently in different sentences.\n\nAn adverb often gives more information about a verb. Many end in -ly, but not all do; some words work differently in different sentences. This matters because a reader or listener can only understand your ideas when you notice how the language works, test your understanding on specific examples and explain the evidence for your choice. Keep the meaning of the original text rather than changing facts to make an answer sound impressive. Read the model closely: which particular word, event or detail changes the meaning? Try explaining the same decision to someone else without repeating the model sentence. Then compare your new attempt with the feedback and revise anything that does not fit the actual evidence.","icon":"📖","notice":"WATCH OUT\nNot every word ending ly is an adverb, and not every adverb ends in ly. Re-read the prompt and correct the misunderstanding before moving on.","title":"Adverbs can explain how, when or where"},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The best approach uses the specific concept, evidence and reasoning before practising independently; copying or inventing unsupported facts does not show understanding. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["Use the example and explain the clue or language choice before trying adverb independently.","Copy every sentence exactly without explaining it.","Add a made-up event and describe it as a fact.","Ignore the task and choose an unrelated detail."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about tense?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WATCH MY THINKING\n\"She loudly shouted\" repeats the loud idea, but \"She whispered urgently\" clarifies a quiet action with a strong reason. I check the past tense whispered before adding urgently.\n\nYOUR SMALL TRY\nImprove \"The driver stopped\" to show that stopping was cautious and happened yesterday.\n\nCOMPARE AND CORRECT\n\"Yesterday the driver stopped carefully\" is clear; slowly or safely could fit another context.","explain":"Choose one useful adverb when it adds information. Check tense before polishing description.\n\nChoose one useful adverb when it adds information. Check tense before polishing description. This matters because a reader or listener can only understand your ideas when you notice how the language works, test your understanding on specific examples and explain the evidence for your choice. Keep the meaning of the original text rather than changing facts to make an answer sound impressive. Read the model closely: which particular word, event or detail changes the meaning? Try explaining the same decision to someone else without repeating the model sentence. Then compare your new attempt with the feedback and revise anything that does not fit the actual evidence.","icon":"📖","notice":"WATCH OUT\nAvoid an adverb that contradicts or simply repeats the verb. Re-read the prompt and correct the misunderstanding before moving on.","title":"Revise for accurate meaning, not adjective clutter"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 3, Lesson 3: Verb tense and adverbs","unit_number":1,"unit_title":"Narrative: story elements and planning","week":3,"week_in_unit":3,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"I DO — \"The possum climbs\" describes what it does now or regularly. \"The possum climbed\" tells a past event. \"The possum went\" is past although it does not end in ed.\n\nNow look for the exact evidence in the words or actions. Draft: \"Mira opened the gate and sees a fox.\" Both actions happened in the story''s past, so I revise: \"Mira opened the gate and saw a fox.\"","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Present tense climbs beside past tense climbed for the same possum action.","caption":"Tense tells whether the action happens now or already happened.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Present</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">The possum climbs</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Past</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">The possum climbed</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Inconsistent sees corrected to saw so both actions stay in the past.","caption":"Keep the main verbs in the same time unless there is a clear reason to shift.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Mixed draft</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">opened the gate and sees a fox</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Consistent past</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">opened the gate and saw a fox</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 3"}') WHERE seed_key='s2-eng-y3-w03-l3' AND family_id IS NULL;
